    excerpt: "Now is a great time to reflect on your season and how you plan to make necessary changes so you are successful in 2015. Categorize your goals: family, health, relationships, finances, career....",
    sourceUrl: "https://t2coaching.com/fitness/t2coaching-tips-to-plan/",
    contentHtml: "<p>Now is a great time to reflect on your season and how you plan to make necessary changes so you are successful in 2015.</p>\n<p>Categorize your goals: family, health, relationships, finances, career. Maybe you decide you want to create more balance (in life and with your strength), train more or less or fine-tune your nutrition. Or maybe you want to focus on more “me” time — whatever works for you. Create and outline daily tasks that you will do each day to get to where you want to be.</p>\n<p>Sit down and answer the following questions:</p>\n<p>• What do you enjoy and what would you change about your health, fitness and sport?</p>\n<p>• What are you goals for the next three, sixand 12 months, as well as five years?</p>\n<p>• How do you plan to reach your goals?</p>\n<p>Your answers will often dictate how you plan to transition into the new year. Maybe you took some time off to let your body rest and are ready to jump into training for your sport again. Maybe you are just starting a more healthy lifestyle and want to stay more active in 2015</p>\n<p>Whether you’re looking to get ready for triathlon season or want to drop a few pounds, with a little planning and preparation, you can achieve your goals, stay healthy this winter and enter the spring 2015 season fit and strong.</p>\n<p>Below are three tips to get you start on setting your resolution/goal:</p>\n<p>52. Create an action plan to achieve your goal.</p>\n<p>3. Overcome mental barriers that keep you from attaining your goal.</p>\n<p>Maybe your New Year’s resolution is to stop starting over and to make healthy nutrition and training your new lifestyle. If you fall off track, you get back on track at your next meal or even next day. Don’t let the snowball effect occur because it turns into an avalanche. Overcome the mind barriers. We have all heard the saying, “if you always do what you always did, then you will always get what you always got.”</p>\n<p>Write down your goal so you know where you are headed, then sit down with a friend or coach to determine if that goal is attainable and realistic based on your experience and availability to prepare for it. One thing I have found useful is to sit down with a calendar. It is easy to set goals, but choosing when you will accomplish them will not only motivate you, it will help you hold yourself accountable. It’s important to put your SMART goals down on paper so that whenever you are struggling on certain days, you can look at your goal sheet to keep you motivated and determined to stay the course.</p>\n<p>Create massive action in the direction of your goals — no one will make your goals happen for you. Action is what gives your goal power. If you do not create change, nothing will happen. Break your goals down into smaller goals and set some objectives. Objectives help create a process for attaining your goal. Small, measurable steps over time allow you to build on your success.</p>\n<p>The bottom line is that there is no quick fix or magic time to live and make your dreams a reality. You owe it to yourself to live each day with your goals in mind. The more days you string together of living as well as possible, the stronger you become, and the more likely you are to achieve your health, fitness and performance goals.</p>\n<p>Wendy Mader is an accomplished triathlete and co-founder and owner of t2 coaching in Fort Collins. Contact her at wendy@t2coaching.com or visit www.t2coaching.com.</p>",
